From: Lukas Klizas <lukas@klizas.lt>
Date: Sun, 5 Jul 2026 14:50:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH v2] brcmfmac: fix suspend failures caused by unresponsive firmware

During suspend, two firmware-dependent paths can stall or fail:

1. brcmf_pcie_pm_enter_D3() sends a D3_INFORM mailbox message to firmware
   and waits 2 seconds for an ACK. If firmware is hung, the timeout returns
   -EIO and aborts the entire system suspend. Fix by proceeding with suspend
   anyway and recording the failure in devinfo->d3_ack_timeout.

2. brcmf_msgbuf_delete_flowring() waits for outstanding_tx to drain before
   checking if the bus is down. During D3 transition, firmware won't process
   pending tx status, causing repeated timeouts across multiple flowrings.
   Fix by checking bus state before and during the wait loop.

v2: v1 relied on brcmf_pcie_pm_leave_D3()'s INTMASK check to detect the
hung firmware on resume and re-probe. In practice a firmware that timed
out on D3_INFORM can still return a nonzero INTMASK, taking the
hot-resume path into a zombie state: the association and DHCP come back
but the data path is dead (observed 2026-07-05 on BCM4364/MacBookPro16,1;
worse, toggling networking in that state can wedge the chip's PCIe
interface and hard-lock the machine). Carry the D3 timeout across the
sleep cycle via devinfo->d3_ack_timeout and force the existing
remove + re-probe path on resume, reloading the firmware.

Signed-off-by: Lukas Klizas <lukas@klizas.lt>
---
diff -ru a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/msgbuf.c b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/msgbuf.c
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/msgbuf.c	2026-07-05 14:47:53.066598047 +0300
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/msgbuf.c	2026-07-05 14:48:40.742060275 +0300
@@ -1410,13 +1410,26 @@
 	int err;
 	int retry = BRCMF_MAX_TXSTATUS_WAIT_RETRIES;
 
+	/* no need to wait or submit if firmware can not be reached */
+	if (drvr->bus_if->state != BRCMF_BUS_UP) {
+		brcmf_dbg(MSGBUF, "bus down, flowring will be removed\n");
+		brcmf_msgbuf_remove_flowring(msgbuf, flowid);
+		return;
+	}
+
 	/* make sure it is not in txflow */
 	brcmf_commonring_lock(commonring_del);
 	flow->rings[flowid]->status = RING_CLOSING;
 	brcmf_commonring_unlock(commonring_del);
 
-	/* wait for commonring txflow finished */
+	/* wait for commonring txflow finished, but bail if bus goes down */
 	while (retry && atomic_read(&commonring_del->outstanding_tx)) {
+		if (drvr->bus_if->state != BRCMF_BUS_UP) {
+			brcmf_dbg(MSGBUF, "bus went down, aborting wait\n");
+			atomic_set(&commonring_del->outstanding_tx, 0);
+			brcmf_msgbuf_remove_flowring(msgbuf, flowid);
+			return;
+		}
 		usleep_range(5000, 10000);
 		retry--;
 	}
@@ -1425,13 +1438,6 @@
 		atomic_set(&commonring_del->outstanding_tx, 0);
 	}
 
-	/* no need to submit if firmware can not be reached */
-	if (drvr->bus_if->state != BRCMF_BUS_UP) {
-		brcmf_dbg(MSGBUF, "bus down, flowring will be removed\n");
-		brcmf_msgbuf_remove_flowring(msgbuf, flowid);
-		return;
-	}
-
 	commonring = msgbuf->commonrings[BRCMF_H2D_MSGRING_CONTROL_SUBMIT];
 	brcmf_commonring_lock(commonring);
 	ret_ptr = brcmf_commonring_reserve_for_write(commonring);
diff -ru a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c	2026-07-05 14:47:53.066526990 +0300
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c	2026-07-05 14:48:40.741885459 +0300
@@ -346,6 +346,7 @@
 	struct brcmf_pcie_shared_info shared;
 	wait_queue_head_t mbdata_resp_wait;
 	bool mbdata_completed;
+	bool d3_ack_timeout;
 	bool irq_allocated;
 	bool wowl_enabled;
 	u8 dma_idx_sz;
@@ -2643,8 +2644,13 @@
 			   BRCMF_PCIE_MBDATA_TIMEOUT);
 	if (!devinfo->mbdata_completed) {
 		brcmf_err(bus, "Timeout on response for entering D3 substate\n");
-		brcmf_bus_change_state(bus, BRCMF_BUS_UP);
-		return -EIO;
+		/* Proceed with suspend anyway. Remember that firmware is
+		 * unresponsive so resume skips the hot-resume path and does
+		 * a full remove + re-probe. Aborting suspend here just strands
+		 * the user with a hung firmware and a machine that won't
+		 * sleep.
+		 */
+		devinfo->d3_ack_timeout = true;
 	}
 
 	devinfo->state = BRCMFMAC_PCIE_STATE_DOWN;
@@ -2666,6 +2672,17 @@
 	devinfo = bus->bus_priv.pcie->devinfo;
 	brcmf_dbg(PCIE, "Enter, dev=%p, bus=%p\n", dev, bus);
 
+	/* Firmware that failed to ACK D3_INFORM at suspend cannot be
+	 * trusted even if its registers still read back sanely: it may
+	 * keep the association up while the data path is dead. Skip the
+	 * hot-resume path and force a full re-probe to reload it.
+	 */
+	if (devinfo->d3_ack_timeout) {
+		devinfo->d3_ack_timeout = false;
+		brcmf_err(bus, "Firmware was unresponsive at suspend, reloading\n");
+		goto cleanup;
+	}
+
 	/* Check if device is still up and running, if so we are ready */
 	if (brcmf_pcie_read_reg32(devinfo, devinfo->reginfo->intmask) != 0) {
 		brcmf_dbg(PCIE, "Try to wakeup device....\n");
